  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The most consequential development of the past 48 hours is the emergence of an agreed MOU text between US and Iranian negotiators. By May 28, both negotiating teams had converged on a document providing for a 60-day ceasefire extension, mine clearance in the Strait of Hormuz, an end to the US naval blockade of Iranian ports, and the opening of a structured nuclear-talks track. Trump was briefed and told intermediaries he needed days, not weeks — but as of the morning of May 30 he had made no announcement. JD Vance's characterization on May 29 — "TBD," with "a couple of language points" outstanding — is the sharpest public-facing data point on where the process stands. Iran's FM Araghchi publicly described US demands as a "mix of truth and lies" on May 30, maintaining Tehran's rhetorical distance while negotiations remain live. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>On the Hormuz question, the proposed MOU contains the formal mechanism that would allow commercial resumption — but the pathway from Trump signature to 50+ vessels per day within a consecutive 7-day window before June 10 requires a compressed operational sequence: presidential sign-off, Iranian mine-disposal verification, insurance market recalibration, and carrier re-activation of Gulf rotations, all within roughly ten days. IMF PortWatch data puts current transits at approximately 4 per day — 4% of the ~95-vessel pre-crisis baseline. Lloyd's hull premiums are confirmed at 5–8% of vessel value, commercially prohibitive and orders of magnitude above the 1.5% threshold required to draw carriers back. One Maersk vessel transited under US Navy escort on May 4 during the brief "Operation Project Freedom" window, which Trump then paused; no carrier has resumed scheduled commercial service, and MSC and Hapag-Lloyd have activated bypass routing plans. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>Lebanon's ceasefire exists as a diplomatic frame around what is operationally a war. Between April 16 and May 26, Israeli aircraft conducted 2,847 recorded strikes on Lebanese territory, killing over 3,151 people by Lebanese Health Ministry count. On May 29, Israeli and Lebanese military delegations held the first direct bilateral military talks in decades at the Pentagon — described as "productive" by DoD — with a fourth political-level round scheduled June 2–3 in Washington. Netanyahu on May 27 ordered the IDF to "intensify blows" against Hezbollah following a drone surge that killed an Israeli soldier. The formal ceasefire framework was not declared void before the May 22 horizon, and the diplomatic channel has expanded in institutional breadth, but the gap between the diplomatic frame and battlefield reality is structural and widening. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Israel's domestic political architecture has advanced procedurally toward elections without yet arriving. The dissolution bill's unanimous 110-0 preliminary reading on May 20 was followed by a Haaretz report on May 24 that the process slowed after Haredi parties accepted Netanyahu's preferred October election timeline. No formal announcement of an early election date earlier than October 27 was made before the June 1 horizon. The first full reading was expected June 1, with three readings required for final dissolution. Netanyahu's calculus is to control the electoral calendar while locking in terms with Haredi coalition partners, not to rush to a ballot date. <span class="cite">F5</span> resolved NO; <span class="cite">N2</span> tracks whether the formal dissolution vote occurs by June 15. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:17%;">Escalation 17%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:47%;">Protracted 47%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:36%;">De-esc 36%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (17%):</strong> If the Trump-Iran MOU fails to materialize — <span style="font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;color:var(--accent)">F3</span> resolves NO — and Lebanon's strike tempo continues its documented trajectory of several hundred Israeli air operations per week, the conflict track has an estimated 17% probability of transitioning from protracted attrition to an escalatory exchange that formally breaks the ceasefire framework.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (47%):</strong> The baseline scenario at 47% is continued war without resolution: Hormuz remains operationally closed to commercial traffic, the Lebanon ceasefire exists on paper while ground and air operations continue, and the Israeli electoral clock advances on a Netanyahu-controlled timeline toward an October ballot, with no actor having the capacity or the political incentive to force a decisive change before mid-June.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (36%):</strong> The 36% de-escalation path runs through Trump signing the MOU within his self-declared "couple of days" window, followed by formal Iranian principal confirmation, initiating a 60-day ceasefire extension and mine-clearance process that — on a lag of one to three weeks — would begin to normalize insurance pricing and allow carriers to reconsider Hormuz routing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media on May 30 is executing a calibrated bifurcation. IRNA and PressTV are broadcasting Araghchi's "mix of truth and lies" formulation not as a withdrawal from talks but as a statement of the Iranian negotiating floor for domestic audiences. The absence of hardliner condemnations from IRGC-affiliated outlets has not been accompanied by public objections from that quarter — a notable absence on a day when the Foreign Minister was publicly distancing himself from US demands. Whether this is the result of institutional coordination or a deliberate information hold while the principal decision remains pending at a higher level cannot be determined from open sources alone. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>The nuclear provisions in the MOU are the most domestically sensitive axis. Iran's negotiating team has proposed a 5-year enrichment moratorium against a reported US demand of 20 years. This gap has not closed publicly. Supreme Leader Khamenei — the Ayatollah's successor Mojtaba Khamenei, installed after Ali Khamenei was killed in the February 28 events that opened the current conflict — has not made a public statement on the MOU text. In Iranian governance terms, this means either that the decision has not been elevated to his office for a final ruling, or that he has chosen to remain off the record while allowing Pezeshkian and Araghchi to manage the announcement optics. These two possibilities have divergent implications for the speed of any formal Iranian confirmation. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Strait of Hormuz remains physically blocked: mines are deployed and IRGC naval assets are present. Any Iranian commitment to reopen Hormuz requires active IRGC cooperation, which is itself a political variable not controlled by the Foreign Ministry. Pezeshkian's government can sign an MOU; the IRGC has to execute the mine clearance. These are not the same actor, and no public reporting as of May 30 confirms that the IRGC command has formally accepted the mine-clearance obligation contained in the negotiated text. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

